== Family 6: Coagulation and Haematological
<sec:um-coagulation>

*Family overview.* The haemostatic system balances clot formation and dissolution via coagulation cascade, platelet activation, and fibrinolysis. Pathological low-level activation produces microclotting, endothelial damage, and chronic inflammation without overt thrombosis.

*Concrete mechanisms and ME/CFS evidence:*

- *Fibrin/amyloid microclot formation.* Platelet-poor plasma from ME/CFS patients contains amyloid fibrin(ogen) microclots at approximately 10× greater area than healthy controls; microclots resist fibrinolysis and may obstruct microvascular perfusion, impairing oxygen delivery to tissues @nunes2022microclots. The abundance of these aggregates is contested: a prospective 151-participant never-hospitalized Long COVID cohort found microaggregate number, area, and fibrin fibre density comparable to recovered controls @Fraser2026LCcoagulation, and the assay has no validated reference standard @Cochrane2023Plasmapheresis. Compositional evidence is the stronger claim: post-translational modification of fibrinogen distinguished post-infectious groups where bulk protein levels did not @Booyens2026POTSPTM.

- *Platelet hyperactivation.* Hyperactivated platelets with increased aggregation propensity; driven by chronic innate immune activation and endothelial injury signals.

- *Hypercoagulable state.* Thromboelastography demonstrates hypercoagulability in a substantial proportion of ME/CFS participants.

- *Red blood cell deformability loss.* Stiffer RBCs with approximately 14% reduced elongation index cannot transit narrow capillaries efficiently, impeding microvascular flow and oxygen delivery (see also Family 3) @marshall2018rbc.

- *Dysregulated coagulation proteome.* Data-independent LC-MS/MS proteomics of ME/CFS plasma reveals dysregulated coagulation system proteins alongside endothelial dysfunction markers @lacerda2024proteomics.

- *Complement-coagulation crosstalk.* Complement C5a amplifies platelet and coagulation activation; provides mechanistic bridge between immune dysregulation (Family 4) and haematological pathology.

Full discussion: shared across @ch:cardiovascular and @ch:immune-dysfunction.

*Evidence status:* Probable (marker-level procoagulant findings are consistent across small cohorts but require larger independent replication; the microaggregate-abundance component is contradicted by a larger prospective cohort and rests on an assay without a validated reference standard; the one large randomised test of anticoagulation in Long COVID showed no fatigue benefit @StimulateICP2026rivaroxaban). Severity applicability: the contradicting cohort was ambulant and never hospitalized, so severe and very-severe ME/CFS remains untested for every readout in this family.

